Under `--modular-termination`, the system module now declares the type `bounded<O, F>` of a bounded callable. `O` is the order, `nat` or `ORDINAL`, and is non-variant. `F` is an arrow type, and the bounded type has exactly its subtyping. A bounded type has no compiled equality, no default value and is not known to be nonempty, and the compilers erase it to `F`. This first milestone has no way to form, apply or project a value. A module's own `bounded`, or an opened import's, hides the system type. Without the option, nothing changes. See https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/266.
